s through to the harness's\n#     built-in default; a malformed section, an unknown arch-map mode or a step\n#     shadowing a built-in name exits loudly instead of being dropped, and a step\n#     whose declared tool is missing is reported as a skip that fails the run\n#     outside the lenient mode.\n#\n# Contract IF-049: the `[run]` section declares the launch capabilities the\n#     run launchers present — one `<name> = <command>` line per capability, in\n#     declaration order, each value a full shell line, plus an optional\n#     `<name>.desc` one-line description. An absent or empty section is the\n#     declared no-capability state: the listing mode prints nothing and exits 0,\n#     while the interactive and direct paths print guidance and exit 1.\n#\n# Contract IF-068: the `[agent-loop]` section declares the session engine's dials\n#     in one home instead of a copy per launcher: `model`, the fallback tier used\n#     when managed routing is off, and `model-map`, the per-phase fallback map\n#     written `PLAN=...,BUILD=...`. Each dial resolves by the precedence CLI flag,\n#     then the matching `AGENT_*` environment slot, then this file, then the\n#     built-in default. A blank value, an absent key, an absent section and an\n#     unreadable file all fall through rather than failing, and a declared\n#     worker-count dial is ignored.\n\n[paths]\nsrc = project-trajectory/scripts\ntests = tests\n\n[product]\nformat = {py} -m ruff format --check {src} {tests}\nlint = {py} -m ruff check {src} {tests}\n# `-n auto` runs the suite across all cores via pytest-xdist (WI-075). Every test\n# is isolated in tmp_path and the meta-tree readers are read-only, so the suite is\n# xdist-safe; subprocess coverage (tests/conftest.augment_env) holds per-worker\n# (measured combined total 90.8%, unchanged from serial). Measured 2026-07-22 on\n# a 24-core box: ~250 s parallel for the full 1374-test suite (~200 s for the\n# smoke tier); the coverage step runs several times longer. These are snapshots,\n# not contracts — re-measure locally before budgeting against them (the prior\n# ~65 s figure predated the v4 dispatch/integrate suite growth and misled\n# session budgeting for weeks; repo-review 2026-07-21 M-24).\n# The `smoke` tier stays as declared; the coverage args below still apply.\ntest = {py} -m pytest -q -n auto\n\n[tiers]\nsmoke = -m smoke\nfull = -m \"not release\"\nrelease =\nall =\n\n[smoke-budget]\n# WI-281: the per-commit smoke bar ([tiers] smoke, run by the session-protocol\n# commit bar) must stay a real smoke test — \"is it basically alive?\" — not a\n# re-run of the full suite. WI-122's opt-out default had inverted: 1088/1378\n# (79%) of tests ran per commit at ~6 min wall. The budget is SPLIT in two,\n# because a wall-clock assert is machine-/core-count-dependent and flaky:\n#   seconds   = the wall-clock target at `-n auto`. The CI wall-clock check\n#               (scripts/check_smoke_budget.py) times the smoke step against it\n#               and ENFORCES it (exit 1 on breach): the re-tiered tier is\n#               startup-dominated, not core-bound (~7.5 s/24 cores, ~10.5 s/2), so\n#               60 s keeps ~5x headroom even on a small runner. `--mode warn` is\n#               the local default (report-only) for convenience.\n#   max-tests = the DETERMINISTIC membership ceiling — always-on, machine-\n#               independent — that tests/test_smoke_budget.py enforces and that\n#               BITES if the tier grows back toward the full suite. This is the\n#               shared floor when the per-lane seconds differ.\n# Both are growth SENSORS with headroom, not exact freezes (new in-process unit\n# tests SHOULD accrue — the WI-122 intent). Re-stamp together, reason in the log.\n# Measured 2026-07-23 (3.11.9, 24 cores): re-tiered smoke = 349 tests / ~7.5 s wall.\n# Re-stamped 2026-07-27 (WI-336/WI-337/WI-338): 450 -> 480. Re-measure with\n#   python -m pytest -q -n auto -m smoke\n# on this box before budge