{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CehQ9NTY71WnshHE2A1fH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"WI-584 is closed and the tree is clean.\n\n## What I did\n\n**Ruled (a):** `refresh_refusal` now judges the registries the act would actually **write**, not the whole ledger.\n\nThe contradiction the row named was real and I reproduced it first, at this tip: `refresh_refusal('.', {low-level-requirements.toml: ref})` returned a refusal listing 17 SR rows and 4 TC rows — every one in a registry `_authorised_registries` excludes, under a header claiming \"nothing in this working tree authorises it,\" which was false.\n\n(a) over (b) because the write set *is* the authority set, so a blocked-but-unwritten registry cannot be absorbed by the act being refused — the block protects nothing. What settled it: the false block isn't an `--approves` peculiarity. A bare no-flag act carrying an approving `Status` move in one registry is refused today by drift in a registry it will never write. Only the general fix reaches that.\n\n**One arm kept unscoped**, deliberately: an act whose write set is **empty** is still judged globally. Pure scoping would have turned the laundering scenario (rewrite an Approved row, then refresh) into a silent exit-0 no-op. The drift survives either way — the writer has been scoped since WI-571 — but silence is the wrong answer, and this is what keeps the original laundering and de-approval tests firing on their own terms rather than being weakened to fit.\n\n**Not vacuous either**, which is the obvious objection: a brand-new row arriving already `Approved` puts its registry in the write set with no `flips` entry, so an unrelated amendment could ride that anchor in. Refused, with its own test.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Six tests, each **confirmed red** against the pre-change module (checked out `65f368e7 -- baseline_snapshot.py`, ran, restored) and green after. Two caveats stated rather than buried in the log: the `copy_NOTHING` test is red only on its new message assertion, and `RESEED`'s red is partly a signature error — its behavioural half is the line above it.\n- One existing test **reworked**, because it pinned the defect: it asserted a refusal *naming* a registry the act cannot write. Its real intent (the pre-WI-571 short-circuit) is now asserted over the **copy**, which is what could actually launder.\n- `check_complexity` went RED on `refresh_refusal` at cognitive 18 from the second decision — measured before/after, so I know it was mine. Cleared by extracting `_refusal_text`; no baseline bump taken.\n- Full suite: `1 failed, 3366 passed, 24 skipped in 630.54s`. Smoke: `1520 passed`, 36.3 s vs the 60 s budget.\n\n**The one failure is inherited, not fixed.** `docs/handoff-2026-09-03.md` is orphaned; re-driven against a detached worktree of the base `794de60d` and the identical single orphan is there. Both available fixes are out of bounds or presumptuous — linking it from a coordination surface this branch may not edit, or adding it to `docs/orphans-allow`, which would quietly overrule the owner's own document.\n\n**Surfaced, not fixed:** `LLR-173` doesn't name the authority gate at all — no `refresh_refusal` in its `CodeSymbol`, and its Detail still says \"Three refusals are load-bearing.\" That's been wrong since 2026-08-20 and WI-571 didn't amend it either. I left it: the row is `Approved`, and adding LLR drift is the opposite of what a row existing to unblock the LLR anchor should do. It wants its own row.\n\nI also committed a small piece of uncommitted WI-589 residue I found in the tree first (its own commit, `WI: WI-589` trailer) so it wasn't mixed into this WI's diff. No spine cell was minted or amended here, so no approval brief is owed, and the re-anchor stays the successor condition for the trunk-side rung."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":516,"cache_rea